  "text": "Allophone: A variant form of a phoneme that occurs in a specific phonological environment.\n\nAnaphora: A word or phrase that refers back to an earlier word or phrase in a sentence.\n\nArticulatory Phonetics: The study of how speech sounds are produced by the vocal tract.\n\nBilingualism: The capacity to speak and understand two languages.\n\nCode-switching: The practice of alternating between two or more languages or language varieties in conversation.\n\nConsonant Cluster: A sequence of consonants that occur together without intervening vowels.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and glides to another.\n\nEjective Consonant: A type of consonant produced by closing the glottis and then pressing air against it.\n\nEuphony: The quality of sounds that are pleasant to the ear.\n\nFalse Friends: Words that look similar in two languages but have different meanings.\n\nGlottal Stop: A stop consonant produced by closing the glottis and then releasing it abruptly.\n\nGrammatical Gender: A classification of nouns based on gender, often with no direct correspondence to biological sex.\n\nHarmony: A phonological process in which sounds in a word or phrase become similar in certain features.\n\nHypercorrection: The use of a more prestigious or formal form than is appropriate in a given context.\n\nInterlanguage: The language system developed by a second language learner, which may have features of both the first and target language.\n\nLaryngeal Consonant: A consonant produced with the larynx, such as /h/ or /\u00e7/.\n\nLexical Stress: The emphasis placed on a particular syllable in a word.\n\nMorpheme: The smallest meaningful unit in a language.\n\nNasal Consonant: A consonant produced with the soft palate lowered so that air can escape through the nose.\n\nPhoneme: The smallest unit of sound that can distinguish meaning in a language.\n\nPhonology: The study of the sound system of a language.\n\nProsody: The rhythm, stress, intonation, and tone patterns in speech.\n\nRegister: A variety of a language used in specific social contexts or for specific purposes.\n\nSemantics: The study of meaning in language.\n\nSoft Palate: The part of the roof of the mouth that can be raised or lowered during speech.\n\nSpeech Prosody: The patterns of stress, intonation, rhythm, and pitch in speech.\n\nStress: The emphasis given to a syllable within a word or phrase.\n\nSynalepha: The phenomenon where two consecutive syllables are pronounced as a single syllable.\n\nTone: A pitch pattern that can distinguish meaning in a language.\n\nVowel Harmony: A phonological process in which vowels in a word are pronounced in a similar way.\n\nWord Stress: The emphasis given to a particular syllable in a word.",
